WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.083
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.383 --> 00:00:09.002
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:17.213
Unit one: Literary Analysis. Today you will add precise, extra information about a text with non-defining 'which' clauses.

4
00:00:17.513 --> 00:00:20.119
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:24.265
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:25.117 --> 00:00:26.118
Narrative.

7
00:00:27.117 --> 00:00:31.717
Narrative means a connected account of events; the story a text tells.

8
00:00:32.317 --> 00:00:34.897
The narrative moves between two timelines.

9
00:00:35.483 --> 00:00:38.159
Your turn. Say: Narrative.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.024
Narrative.

11
00:00:42.783 --> 00:00:43.831
Symbolism.

12
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:49.591
Symbolism means the use of objects or images to represent abstract ideas.

13
00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:53.054
The novel's symbolism links water with memory.

14
00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:56.314
Your turn. Say: Symbolism.

15
00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:00.237
Symbolism.

16
00:01:00.983 --> 00:01:01.926
Motif.

17
00:01:02.917 --> 00:01:06.762
Motif means an image or idea that recurs and develops a theme.

18
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.675
The broken clock is a recurring motif.

19
00:01:10.283 --> 00:01:12.448
Your turn. Say: Motif.

20
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.255
Motif.

21
00:01:17.017 --> 00:01:18.424
Characterization.

22
00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:23.460
Characterization means the methods a writer uses to build a character.

23
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.257
Subtle characterization makes the villain sympathetic.

24
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.769
Your turn. Say: Characterization.

25
00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:35.018
Characterization.

26
00:01:35.783 --> 00:01:36.738
Allegory.

27
00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:41.839
Allegory means a story whose characters and events stand for a hidden meaning.

28
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.844
Animal Farm is a political allegory.

29
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.951
Your turn. Say: Allegory.

30
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:51.871
Allegory.

31
00:01:52.617 --> 00:01:53.827
Ambiguity.

32
00:01:54.817 --> 00:01:58.349
Ambiguity means the quality of being open to more than one reading.

33
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.542
The ending's ambiguity divides critics.

34
00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.849
Your turn. Say: Ambiguity.

35
00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:08.736
Ambiguity.

36
00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:10.264
Tone.

37
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.387
Tone means the writer's attitude as expressed through style.

38
00:02:14.983 --> 00:02:17.192
The tone shifts from playful to bitter.

39
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:20.006
Your turn. Say: Tone.

40
00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:23.636
Tone.

41
00:02:24.383 --> 00:02:25.675
Interpretation.

42
00:02:26.683 --> 00:02:30.030
Interpretation means an explanation of what a text means.

43
00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:33.301
Her interpretation is supported by close reading.

44
00:02:33.917 --> 00:02:36.697
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

45
00:02:39.533 --> 00:02:40.883
Interpretation.

46
00:02:41.617 --> 00:02:42.606
Foreshadowing.

47
00:02:43.617 --> 00:02:46.696
Foreshadowing means hints that anticipate later events.

48
00:02:47.283 --> 00:02:50.026
The storm in chapter one is clever foreshadowing.

49
00:02:50.617 --> 00:02:53.466
Your turn. Say: Foreshadowing.

50
00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:57.487
Foreshadowing.

51
00:02:58.250 --> 00:02:59.170
Irony.

52
00:03:00.183 --> 00:03:04.099
Irony means a gap between what is said or expected and what is true.

53
00:03:04.683 --> 00:03:07.229
The irony is that the doctor falls ill first.

54
00:03:07.817 --> 00:03:10.202
Your turn. Say: Irony.

55
00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:13.998
Irony.

56
00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.462
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:19.317 --> 00:03:25.359
Visal, your essay on the novel argues that the broken clock is its key motif. Why that image in particular?

58
00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:34.696
Because the clock, which stops on the day the mother leaves, reappears every time a character refuses to move on.

59
00:03:35.783 --> 00:03:39.153
So it works on a symbolic level rather than a literal one.

60
00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:49.787
Exactly. And the final chapter, in which the clock is finally repaired, suggests that healing is possible, although the tone remains ambiguous.

61
00:03:50.883 --> 00:03:55.033
Some readers find that ending rather sentimental. How would you respond?

62
00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:05.197
I'd concede it is hopeful, but the irony of the narrator's last line, which undercuts that hope, keeps it from being simplistic.

63
00:04:06.333 --> 00:04:08.054
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:08.917 --> 00:04:11.857
Grammar focus: Relative clauses with which.

65
00:04:12.157 --> 00:04:13.320
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:14.017 --> 00:04:17.595
The motif, which repeats throughout, reveals deeper meaning.

67
00:04:18.483 --> 00:04:23.478
The final chapter, in which the clock is repaired, suggests that healing is possible.

68
00:04:24.383 --> 00:04:27.973
The ending is ambiguous, which has divided critics for decades.

69
00:04:28.933 --> 00:04:42.629
A non-defining clause adds extra information inside commas; never use 'that' here. 'Which' can also refer to a whole clause: '…, which divided critics'. In formal writing, use 'in which / of which' rather than ending with a preposition.

70
00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:48.439
Be careful! Don't say: The clock, that stops early, is a motif.

71
00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.410
Say: The clock, which stops early, is a motif.

72
00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.324
Never use 'that' in a non-defining clause.

73
00:04:57.133 --> 00:04:59.937
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:02.303
Reading Beneath the Surface.

75
00:05:02.603 --> 00:05:07.947
Literary analysis begins where summary ends, with the question of how a text creates meaning.

76
00:05:08.933 --> 00:05:14.613
A motif, which may be as small as a colour or a sound, gains significance each time it returns.

77
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:22.093
Characterization is rarely stated directly; instead, it emerges from dialogue, action and the narrator's tone.

78
00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.223
Ambiguity, which some readers find frustrating, often invites the richest interpretation.

79
00:05:29.233 --> 00:05:34.611
Ultimately, a convincing analysis anchors every claim in specific evidence from the text.

80
00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:40.027
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:40.883 --> 00:05:42.558
Choose the correct sentence.

82
00:05:42.858 --> 00:05:52.142
A, The clock, that stops early, is a motif.. B, The clock, which stops early, is a motif.. C, The clock which, stops early is a motif..

83
00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:59.789
The answer is B: The clock, which stops early, is a motif..

84
00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:04.052
The ending is ambiguous, blank has divided critics.

85
00:06:04.352 --> 00:06:07.286
A, which. B, that. C, what.

86
00:06:10.833 --> 00:06:12.345
The answer is A: which.

87
00:06:13.483 --> 00:06:17.143
Hints in early chapters that anticipate later events are called blank.

88
00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:21.398
A, allegory. B, irony. C, foreshadowing.

89
00:06:24.933 --> 00:06:27.072
The answer is C: foreshadowing.

90
00:06:28.217 --> 00:06:30.216
Which is the most formal version?

91
00:06:30.516 --> 00:06:37.862
A, the chapter in which the clock is repaired. B, the chapter the clock gets fixed in. C, the chapter where they fix the clock up.

92
00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:45.164
The answer is A: the chapter in which the clock is repaired.

93
00:06:46.317 --> 00:06:50.197
According to the reading, where does characterization usually come from?

94
00:06:50.497 --> 00:06:56.565
A, direct statements by the author. B, dialogue, action and tone. C, the book's title.

95
00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:02.692
The answer is B: dialogue, action and tone.

96
00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.017
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:07.883 --> 00:07:10.347
How does symbolism enrich a story's meaning?

98
00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:28.546
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:28.846 --> 00:08:06.301
In my view, symbolism enriches a story because it lets a single image carry ideas that would sound heavy if stated directly. A reader who notices a recurring symbol, which may be as simple as a broken clock, begins to read more actively. For example, in many novels water symbolises both danger and renewal, which creates tension. Admittedly, some readers feel symbols are over-interpreted in class. Even so, I believe symbolism rewards close reading and makes a story memorable.

100
00:08:06.601 --> 00:08:08.125
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:09.017 --> 00:08:13.410
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and relative clauses with which.

102
00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:17.129
Today's value is love of reading. Read a book every day.

103
00:08:17.429 --> 00:08:20.023
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

104
00:08:20.323 --> 00:08:24.102
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

